# Chromia MCP - the working agreement

This repository is the Chromia MCP server. The standard is the owner's, in his words:
**the best possible MCP for an agent - everything real, battle tested, not fake.**

Read, in this order, before changing anything:

1. `GOAL.md` - the principles and the round-by-round record. Every claim in it is
   testable or deleted; six test classes read it.
2. `docs/AGENT-LANE-BRIEF.md` - how a lane works: its own worktree, its own
   database, the shared build slot, the gate.
3. `docs/ADVERSARY-ROUND-BRIEF.md` - how a round attacks the server and pins what it finds.
4. `app/src/test/resources/exploit-corpus/CORPUS.md` - the scoreboard; read it rather
   than any prose about coverage.
5. `README.md` "Testing Layers" - what the suite proves and how.

Rules that are not negotiable:

- **No test doubles.** No mocks, fakes, stubs, recording repositories, mock HTTP
  engines, anonymous implementations of production types, delegation or proxies
  in tests. `NoTestDoublesTest` scans for them. A third-party path is tested
  LIVE (explorer, docs site, chain, `chr`, embedder); an upstream outage makes the
  suite red and the remedy is retry, never a skip. A test that cannot be made
  real is deleted together with the claim it carried.
- **No skips.** The gate is zero-skip and has no allowlist. Environment-gated
  live tests go through `LiveEnv` and FAIL when the environment is enabled but the
  resource is broken. `local-test-env.properties` (gitignored) must enable the
  live environment or the gate refuses to run.
- **Never push unbuilt or ungated.** The merge gate is
  `node scripts/loop-gate.mjs --dir <repo> --expect-min <last verified count>`
  (the count is the last verified tally, never an estimate). A docs-only change
  uses `--docs-only --base <commit>`, which DERIVES the test classes from the
  diff - never a hand-picked list. On a host that cannot finish the suite inside
  the test task's cap, run it as partitions and tally the union against the class
  list on disk; say so in the record.
- **One build at a time on this machine.** Before any Gradle run, check that no
  `java.exe` with `gradle-wrapper.jar|GradleWorkerMain|Gradle Test Executor` in its
  command line is running, and that free memory is sufficient. Never
  `gradlew --stop` (it kills someone else's build). Never edit `app/` or
  `GOAL.md` while your own build runs.
- **A verification tool proves canonical shapes and refuses the rest.** A
  heuristic over runner output loses one token away every round (rounds 11-18).
  `verify_guards` answers `ambiguous_refusal`, never `ok:true`, for what it cannot
  prove.
- **Resolve a name the way the compiler resolves it, never a superset.** Round 19
  made namespaces work by filing every helper under every SUFFIX of its namespace
  path, reasoning that a wider name can only cost an `ambiguous_refusal`. It cost
  a false `ok:true` one declaration later: the suffix reached the wrong body (a
  local `namespace h` shadowing an exact import) and two bodies at once. A model
  that accepts spellings the language does not have is not conservative, it is
  wrong in both directions - measure the order (`chr test`), then implement it.
- **Every guard has a mutant** that reddens a shipped must-fail test BECAUSE the
  attack landed, in both the `src/` and flat layouts. A mutant that goes red for
  another reason proves nothing.
- **Evidence is frozen.** Adversary recorders write to `app/build/` and assert
  equality with the committed recording; re-freezing is a deliberate commit with
  the reason. Never overwrite committed evidence from a test run.
- **Nothing tracked points at a machine.** No absolute local paths, user names,
  session ids, temp directories or compiled caches (`NoLocalMachinePathsTest`).
  The repository is public.
- **No credentials anywhere.** Never commit, paste, or type a key, token or
  password; never accept terms or create accounts on the owner's behalf.
- **Tool descriptions stay under 1,200 bytes**; the long form lives in
  `describe_tool`. Retire a superseded sentence in `ToolDescriptionBudgetTest`
  with the reason - never delete it silently.
- **Templates make bugs unwritable** (principle 4): when no precise rule exists,
  ship the shape that cannot express the bug, with the drain as a must-fail test.
  A redirect to a template that does not cover the ask's exploit class is a hazard;
  name the declined class and the missing guard instead.
- **A DESIGN NOTE IS PRODUCTION ADVICE WITH NO TEST BEHIND IT** (round 20). Agents
  read `docs/TEMPLATE-GAPS.md` and BUILD from it. Round 20 built that file's own
  commit-reveal raffle note, proved eleven guards load-bearing on a chain, and was
  drained five of five draws on 4.76% of the stake - because one sentence in the
  note ("her choice is between REVEALING and FORFEITING her deposit") is true of one
  commitment and false of two. Write the note's INVARIANT as something a chain can
  be made to answer, not as a sentence, or do not write the note.
- **A class is recognised when the ask is ABOUT it, not when its word appears**
  (round 20). `DappScaffold.closestTemplateNote` gives each concept class a role
  from WHERE its keys landed - inside the head noun phrase (answer the ask with it),
  named after the head (a compound ask: name every covered half, scaffold none), or
  a merely WEAK key after the head (a note beside the scaffold, never a refusal in
  place of it) - and a key inside a negation window is the class being ruled out.
  Seven asks for classes this server SHIPS A TEMPLATE FOR were refused before that
  rule, including a stablecoin ask whose own words were that it uses no VRF. Add a
  key only with the corpus row that proves it fires and the clean-pass ask that
  proves it does not steal.

Running the server from Cursor: the `chromia-local` MCP entry in `~/.cursor/mcp.json`
starts the local jar over stdio (no port, no token). Rebuild it with
`gradlew shadowJar` after a merge; the jar is `app/build/libs/chromia-mcp-server.jar`.
